/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The 1999 reform strengthened the sustainability, effectiveness, and efficiency of the pension system by introducing a defined-contribution structure that improved transparency, actuarial fairness, work incentives, and long-term financial stability. However, it also increased inequality and lowered replacement rates, raising the risk of future old-age poverty.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
532 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The 2014 reform focused on short-term fiscal relief by reducing the importance of the funded pillar. Although it temporarily improves public finances, it undermines long-term sustainability, lowers national savings, slows economic growth, and only slightly enhances redistribution, making it ineffective and inefficient over time.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
533 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Overall, the 1999 reform is economically efficient but socially costly, whereas the 2014 reform is fiscally driven, only partly effective in the short term, and inefficient in the long term. Future reforms must balance sustainability and fairness through stronger solidarity mechanisms, better pension adequacy, and greater institutional stability.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
